hey everyone this is kevin from the  chess website comm and today we're gonna  be going over a chest tactic that  everyone needs to be aware of and that  is the blockade and that typically is  going to refer to stopping your opponent  pushing pawns to advantageous squares  many times that's gonna be a passed pawn  but it's not always gonna be a passed  pawn so we'll look at different examples  of what that would look like in this  case white does have a passed pawn here  on e5 they're gonna be looking to push  that up the board they do have support  with their queen and their rook and if  you look from black standpoint two  things to keep in mind one black does  need to stop this pawn from pushing  forward but it also has different  options you can bring us look here to e6  you can bring us Queen here to e6 and  you can also bring your knight here to  e6 so one you need to always be looking  for blockades and the second one is  understanding the best pieces to use for  a blockade and Knights generally you're  going to be the best especially if you  compare those two rook saying Queens  especially for a passed pawn this is not  only blocking the pond but also the  knight does a good job of being able to  hop over pieces it can still control a  lot of the board even being here on e6  if the rook is here on e6 it can't  really control anything else it's one of  your major pieces and you want that  doing other things on the board well  your night here on e6 doing a good job  of blocking this but still defending and  attacking some of the critical squares  in the center of the board here you're  threatening if this bishop moves you can  come and attack this pawn here on f4 if  you ever need to you can swing over here  to d4 but also just protecting some  other squares on the center of the board  what your rook wouldn't be able to do  and you want your Queen more in the  attacking lanes controlling a lot more  of the board than just being a blockade  sometimes you have to use the Queen but  not always so passed pawns you're  usually gonna be looking to bring your  knight there because the last thing you  want to do is have your opponent start  pushing your pawn up the boar their pawn  up the board and then you're pretty much  stuck you don't have a lot of options  except to put everything behind that to  stop it going up maybe one or two  squares to promote but  every time that you're not gonna be  stopping a passed pawn sometimes you're  gonna be looking just to stop a pawn  from moving its first move in the game  this is a position from a game in the  early 1900s from Nozawa CH who's one of  the the fathers of the hyper modern  openings in chess but had a lot to do  with thinking about the the theory  behind different chess tactics blockade  being one of those that he spent a lot  of time on and in this position he was  white and he recognized that he did not  want to have to deal with black having  any sort of queenside advantage and so  he looked at this and while there's no  past pawns that he had to worry about he  never wanted this pawn to push forward  to a5 which he thought would be a pain  that he would have to deal with maybe  not necessarily right away but at some  point so when he looked at this  he said Bishop to a6 is a great blockade  it stopped this pawn so remember we just  looked at a previous example where we're  stopping a past pun but it's not always  that you need to be thinking about what  pawns your opponent has that you do not  want them to move other pieces on the  board besides the pawn they can move  backwards they can move anywhere they  want to pawns can only move forward and  they can't capture in front of them and  so right in front of them that is and so  we can always be looking at ways to stop  them from moving where they want to now  this limits where this pawn can go to  and it limits some of the squares and  the options that black has on the Queen  side of the board  now beyond finding the best blockade  against your opponent equally is  important to figure out how to stop your  opponent from blockading your own setup  on the board so in this case it's  White's move and you can see white has a  passed pawn here on d6 but we have this  annoying night here on d7 which is  correctly blockaded this pawn right here  but white has a pretty easy job in this  position we'll look at some others that  are not as easy to get rid of this  Knight so the first move would be Knight  to d3 preparing for the knight to come  to c5 and then remove that night there's  no real good square for black to go  he could play King to geez  seven followed by Knight to c5 now black  can't do anything if he moves this night  this pawns just going to push forward  and then it's gonna promote here to d8  and get a queen you can't stop that if  black tries to just capture here the  King's gonna recapture this King is that  now I'm gonna gobble up all these pawns  on the Queen side and have a pretty easy  game to push forward black can't stop it  so I'll may look like even in material  and black has the blockade White's gonna  be looking to find the way to remove  that piece right here at the blockade  and then continue to push the piece  wherever he is trying to do in this case  it's a passed pawn other cases in the  previous example it may just be trying  to get that pawned pushing forward on  the Queen side so he has some sort of  air to to breathe on the Queen side  maybe just pushing a7 to a5 is all black  is trying to do to start to attack his  opponent now that we've covered the  concept of a blockade we can take a look  at a famous game and see how this is  used in practice so this is from 1950s  between Mac sube he was a world champion  versus his opponent Herman Phil Nick  he's playing with the black pieces and  you can see white has a passed pawn  looking to push this on the D file has  some protection behind it with the Queen  and blacks trying to stop that and so he  starts with the move Knight to c4  preparing for 90 d6 and this is  hopefully after a few examples we've  looked at you can see that this is what  black is trying to do now max is trying  to stop this Knight from coming up here  and trying to find in a way to push  forward and he has a few options that he  was looking at here the first one would  see Knight to be 5 this is stopping the  immediate threat of the knight coming to  d6 because then then I could take you  see an exchange and you know white  doesn't really mind if the Queen  stopping this a Knights a much better  Square to stop a passed pawn as we've  talked about if your opponent is using  your their Queen to stop your pawn from  pushing forward that feels like a huge  win so you can start to explore this  little warrants a knight to b5 looks  promising but then you can see that  night to c7 all that kind of stops it  because this is attacking the knight  that and I can't come to DC  right now cuz it would just fall so  what's kind of the waiting move maybe a  for to protect this but then after the  night takes and the pond recaptures you  can't stop the night coming up here to  d6 another option if we look from white  standpoint of how to continue you could  see f4 and you're almost hoping that the  pond takes here and eventually you can  push forward with E 5 at some point and  then push forward with a d6 this seems  like a possibility but black doesn't  have to take instead you could see black  just immediately come up here on d6 and  if the pawn captures the bishop can  recapture here on e5 it's still very  difficult for white to stop what black  is doing now max in the game decided to  try something different looked at those  options and said don't don't think  either those are going to work so  instead he tried to move he started with  the move  b3 now this does not stop the immediate  threat of the knight coming back here to  d6 so he continued with Bishop here to  e3 and then we see pawn to b6 so let it  find the pawn chain on the Queen side of  the board Queen to d2 preparing for the  rook to come over then we work over here  to e8 and then upon to f4 now black  already has the blockade here on d6 now  it's a matter of holding on to the  position so he starts with bringing his  Knight here to c7 definitely doesn't  want it here on the edge here on the a  file so then rook to f2 we see the pawn  take on f4 Bishop takes on f4 Bishop  comes down here to a6 trying to add a  little bit more pressure also opens up  for his rook here on a a to get involved  into the game and then we see rook  swinging over here to e1 now if white  looks at this and says now that my  bishops here on f4 we could actually  take the night here on d6 now this may  look simply good but long-term blacks  still going to have a very good game so  Bishop takes here on d6 and then the  Queen takes here on d6 you could say  well yeah that's exactly what white  would be looking to do and and yes in  the short term but then maybe rook to c1  protecting at this Knight  getting it to a semi-open file but then  rook to e7 and this is preparing for the  night to come here to to e8 so even if  the Queen came to f4 trying to kick this  Queen off  Knight to e eighths and then after if  the Queen's exchange that's fine but now  the knight can easily come to d6 in the  next move and so you can see that black  is still able to control with his rook  with his Queen and then his knight  coming here as well controlling both the  d6 square vitally important to stop the  past pawn from pushing as well as this  square here on e5 as well and this is a  blockade that doesn't have a piece right  away but it is being protected by both  of the rook and the bishop right now  here on e5 so it's stopping both of  these pawns here in the center of the  board  using some some very good moves as well  as just tactics against a world champion  now black because of how he played  specifically with his blockade and  stopping the push in the center the  board went on to win this game so I  thought this would be a good example to  show it in practice of looking at the  board seeing what your pony's trying to  do see if they have pawns that you need  to stop them whether it's a passed pawn  on side of the board or if you just look  at and say hey this pawn is gonna be a  nuisance so I need to stop that at all  cost because maybe if it moves forward  it's gonna kick one of my pieces it's on  a great outpost out out of the way and I  don't want that to happen so always be  looking in your games for ways to stop  your opponent's pawns from pushing  forward to squares that you don't want  them to go so hopefully you guys enjoyed  this video on nice little chess tactic  the blockade if you guys have other  strategy or tactics other videos you  want me to make feel free to let me know  best way to reach me is on the website  the chess website calm you can always  chat with me directly there's a chat  functionality on the website you can  always email me at the chess website at  gmail.com but thank you guys so much for  watching I'll see you guys the next  video  you

